    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, what do u mean by a goblin? i don't get how that helps explain the pressure difference. can u just explain the physics part again without the metaphors? i just need to understand the actual airflow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me break it down for you in a more straightforward way.\n\nWhen an airfoil, or wing, moves through the air, it creates a region of lower pressure above the wing and a region of higher pressure below. This is due to the conservation of energy and momentum. As the wing moves forward, it deflects the air downward, creating a swirling motion behind it. This swirling motion creates a region of lower pressure above the wing, as the air is moving faster and has less kinetic energy.\n\nAt the same time, the air flowing over the top surface of the wing has to travel faster to keep up with the wing's motion, which creates a region of higher pressure above the wing. Conversely, the air flowing along the bottom surface of the wing has to travel slower, which creates a region of lower pressure below the wing.\n\nThis pressure difference creates an upward force, or lift, that counteracts the weight of the plane. The shape of the wing is such that it deflects the air downward, creating a longer path for the air to follow, which results in a greater pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces.\n\nAs for Newton's third law, it's simply a statement",
      "source": "target",
